Next, the method handles concurrent updates by beginning a transaction with the database. This ensures that only one user can update their password at a time, preventing race conditions and data integrity issues. The method then updates the password in the database using a prepared statement, binding the parameters to avoid SQL injection attacks.

After updating the password, the method logs the password change attempt using the `logPasswordChangeAttempt()` method. This method inserts a new row into the `password_change_attempts` table with the user ID, old password (hashed), and new password (hashed). The log is used for auditing purposes and can be queried to track password change attempts over time.

