Next, the function generates a random 3-digit number and appends it to the base name. It then constructs a new filename by replacing the original extension with a new one determined by the first two characters of the base name. If the first two characters are not alphanumeric, the default extension is used instead.

The function validates the proposed filename against a list of reserved filenames to avoid conflicts. It handles collisions by appending a unique identifier generated from the current timestamp.

Finally, the function ensures the total length of the new filename does not exceed 128 characters, truncating if necessary, but prioritizing readability over strict length constraints. The function manages potential memory allocation failures during string manipulation and handles them gracefully, returning `NULL` in case of failure.

