The `addRecord` method adds a new record to the memory manager by allocating a new `Record` structure on the heap and storing it in a vector of pointers. The `processRecords` method invokes a callback function for each record in the memory manager, passing the pointer to the record as an argument.

The program also defines a static member variable `gen` to hold a random number generator that is used to populate the integer fields of the records within a specified range. The `uniform_int_distribution` function is used to generate random integers within the specified range.

In the `main` function, an instance of the `MemoryManager` class is created with 1000 records and a maximum value of 100. Some records are added to the memory manager using the `addRecord` method, and then the `processRecords` method is called to process all the records using a callback function that prints each record to the console.

This program demonstrates how to implement a flexible memory management framework for initializing multiple data structures to support a data processing pipeline in C++. The framework supports dynamic memory allocation and deallocation, and can handle an unspecified number of records with different data types. The program also includes a high-quality random number generator to avoid race conditions in concurrent environments.']

