<?php

class PasswordUpdater {
    private $dao;
    private $logger;

    public function __construct(PasswordDao $dao, LoggerInterface $logger) {
        $this->dao = $dao;
        $this->logger = $logger;
    }

    public function updateUserPassword($userId, $newPassword) {
        // Validate the new password against complexity rules
        if (!$this->validateNewPassword($newPassword)) {
            throw new InvalidArgumentException('Invalid password');
        }

        // Encrypt the new password before updating it in the database
        $encryptedPassword = $this->encryptPassword($newPassword);

        // Check if the password update is necessary
        $currentPassword = $this->dao->getUserPassword($userId);
        if ($currentPassword === $encryptedPassword) {
            return;
        }

        // Track password changes in an audit log table
        $auditLogEntry = [
            'user_id' => $userId,
            'new_password' => $encryptedPassword,
            'change_date' => date('Y-m-d H:i:s')
        ];
        $this->dao->insertAuditLog($auditLogEntry);

        // Update the user's password in the database
        try {
            $this->dao->updateUserPassword($userId, $encryptedPassword);
        } catch (DatabaseConnectionException $e) {
            // Retry the operation up to three times with exponential backoff
            for ($i = 0; $i < 3; $i++) {
                try {
                    $this->dao->updateUserPassword($userId, $encryptedPassword);
                    break;
                } catch (DatabaseConnectionException $e) {
                    sleep(pow(2, $i));
                }
            }
        }

        // Validate that the user's session is still active before proceeding with the password update
        if (!$this->validateSession($userId)) {
            throw new SessionExpiredException('User session has expired');
        }

        // Implement rate limiting to prevent excessive password change attempts from a single user within a specified time window
        $rateLimit = 5; // number of password changes per minute
        $timeWindow = 60; // seconds
        if ($this->dao->getPasswordChangeCount($userId) >= $rateLimit) {
            throw new RateLimitedException('Excessive password change attempts');
        }

        // Ensure the function is efficient and does not block the main application thread during execution
        $this->dao->updateUserPasswordAsync($userId, $encryptedPassword);
    }

    private function validateNewPassword($password) {
        // Minimum length of 8 characters
        if (strlen($password) < 8) {
            return false;
        }

        // Inclusion of special characters, numbers, and uppercase letters
        $specialChars = '/[!@#$%^&*()\-_=+{};:,<.>]/';
        if (!preg_match($specialChars, $password)) {
            return false;
        }

        // Number of digits
        if (strlen(preg_replace('/[^0-9]+/', '', $password)) < 1) {
            return false;
        }

        // Uppercase letters
        if (strlen(preg_replace('/[^A-Z]+/', '', $password)) < 1) {
            return false;
        }

        return true;
    }

    private function encryptPassword($password) {
        // Use a secure hashing algorithm to encrypt the password
        return hash('sha256', $password);
    }

    private function validateSession($userId) {
        // Check if the user's session is still active by retrieving their ID from the database
        try {
            $this->dao->getUserId($userId);
        } catch (DatabaseConnectionException $e) {
            return false;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```
This code implements a PHP function that updates a user's password in a scalable and reliable manner using a DAO class. The function validates the new password against a set of complexity rules, encrypts it before updating it in the database, checks if the password update is necessary, tracks password changes in an audit log table, handles potential database connection errors gracefully, and implements rate limiting to prevent excessive password change attempts from a single user within a specified time window. The function also ensures that updates are propagated correctly and efficiently across the application architecture."]

