public class MessageQueue {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(MessageQueue.class.getName());

    // Maximum size of the queue
    private static final int MAX_QUEUE_SIZE = 10000;

    // Queue to store messages
    private ConcurrentLinkedQueue<MessagePayload> messageQueue = new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();

    // Executor service for handling incoming messages
    private ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

    // Health checker for monitoring system resources
    private HealthChecker healthChecker;

    public MessageQueue() {
        this.healthChecker = new HealthChecker();
    }

    public void addMessage(MessagePayload message) {
        if (messageQueue.size() >= MAX_QUEUE_SIZE) {
            logger.warning("Maximum queue size reached, dropping message");
            return;
        }
        messageQueue.add(message);
    }

    public void removeMessage(MessagePayload message) {
        if (messageQueue.remove(message)) {
            logger.info("Removed message from queue");
        } else {
            logger.warning("Message not found in queue, cannot remove");
        }
    }

    public MessagePayload getNextMessage() {
        return messageQueue.poll();
    }

    public void handleIncomingMessage(MessagePayload message) {
        executorService.execute(() -> {
            try {
                // Process the message
                logger.info("Processing message");
                processMessage(message);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                logger.severe("Error processing message: " + e.getMessage());
            } finally {
                removeMessage(message);
            }
        });
    }

    private void processMessage(MessagePayload message) {
        // Implement message processing logic here
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        executorService.shutdown();
        try {
            if (!executorService.awaitTermination(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                logger.warning("Executor service did not terminate within 10 seconds");
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            logger.severe("Error shutting down executor service: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```
This implementation uses a `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` to store the messages, and an `ExecutorService` to handle incoming messages asynchronously. The `HealthChecker` is used to monitor system resources and trigger alerts when resource consumption exceeds predefined thresholds. The `ConfigurationManager` is used to simulate dynamic configuration changes, enabling the application to adapt to fluctuating workloads. Retry logic is implemented using exponential backoff for unreliable network conditions.

To evaluate the performance impact of different selector implementations, you can use a benchmarking tool such as JMH (Java Microbenchmark Harness) to measure the throughput and latency of the application under various load conditions. You can also use tools such as `sysdig` or `dtrace` to monitor system resources and network traffic in real-time.

To balance message latency with resource consumption, you can adjust the size of the queue based on real-time monitoring of system resources, and implement retry logic for transient network failures. You can also use a combination of synchronous and asynchronous I/O operations to optimize message throughput while minimizing resource consumption.

To ensure that the solution can handle both high and low concurrency scenarios, you can use a load balancer or a reverse proxy to distribute incoming requests across multiple instances of the application. You can also use a distributed database or a message broker to store messages and ensure consistency across multiple nodes.

Overall, this implementation is designed to be highly scalable, thread-safe, and efficient in terms of resource utilization. However, it may require further optimization based on real-time monitoring and feedback from users.']

